---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CalcMoneyVat from '@/components/tools/finance/CalcMoneyVat.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="vat-calculator">
  <CalcMoneyVat client:load variant="vat" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Adding VAT and removing VAT</h2>
    <p>
      Adding is easy: multiply the net figure by 1 plus the rate. At the UK standard rate of 20%,
      100 net becomes 120 gross.
    </p>
    <p>
      To remove VAT, divide by 1.2; do not subtract 20%. 120 minus 20% is 96; 120 divided by 1.2 is the correct
      answer, 100. The error is 4 units in every hundred, because a percentage of the larger number differs
      from a percentage of the smaller one.
    </p>

    <h2>The VAT fraction</h2>
    <p>
      The tax share of a VAT-inclusive price is <code>rate ÷ (100 + rate)</code>. HMRC calls it the VAT
      fraction. For the UK standard rate it is 20/120 = 1/6, so one sixth of any standard-rated gross price is
      VAT.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[420px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Rate</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Divide gross by</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">VAT fraction</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">VAT in 1,000 gross</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>5%</td><td style="text-align:right">1.05</td><td style="text-align:right">1/21</td><td style="text-align:right">47.62</td></tr>
          <tr><td>19%</td><td style="text-align:right">1.19</td><td style="text-align:right">19/119</td><td style="text-align:right">159.66</td></tr>
          <tr><td>20%</td><td style="text-align:right">1.20</td><td style="text-align:right">1/6</td><td style="text-align:right">166.67</td></tr>
          <tr><td>21%</td><td style="text-align:right">1.21</td><td style="text-align:right">21/121</td><td style="text-align:right">173.55</td></tr>
          <tr><td>23%</td><td style="text-align:right">1.23</td><td style="text-align:right">23/123</td><td style="text-align:right">186.99</td></tr>
          <tr><td>25%</td><td style="text-align:right">1.25</td><td style="text-align:right">1/5</td><td style="text-align:right">200.00</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Standard rates around Europe</h2>
    <p>
      The VAT calculator presets cover the standard and common reduced rates for the UK, Ireland, Germany,
      France, the Netherlands, Spain, Italy, Belgium, Poland, Sweden, Denmark, Portugal, Austria, Switzerland
      and South Africa. They are stored in the page with the date they were checked, because this site makes no
      network requests.
    </p>
    <p>
      A static table does not update itself. Rates change rarely, and changes are announced in advance. Check
      with your own tax authority before you file anything, and type a custom rate when you need one that is
      not listed.
    </p>

    <h2>Reduced, zero-rated and exempt</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Reduced rate: a lower positive rate on specified goods, such as domestic energy or children's car
        seats in the UK at 5%.
      </li>
      <li>
        Zero-rated: VAT is charged at 0%. It is still a taxable supply, so you can reclaim input VAT on the
        costs behind it. In the UK, most food and children's clothing are zero-rated.
      </li>
      <li>
        Exempt: outside the VAT system. No VAT is charged and input VAT generally cannot be reclaimed.
        Examples are insurance, most financial services, and much education and healthcare.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Zero-rated and exempt look the same to the customer and are very different for the business. Whether you
      can reclaim input VAT depends on this distinction.
    </p>

    <h2>Invoicing several lines</h2>
    <p>
      The multi-line panel handles a real invoice: any number of lines, each with its own rate, so a mix of
      standard-rated services and zero-rated goods totals correctly. Each line is rounded to the minor unit
      separately, as almost all accounting software does and most tax authorities expect. Rounding only the
      total can differ by a penny per line; that is normally acceptable, but be consistent.
    </p>
    <p>
      A compliant VAT invoice needs more than the arithmetic: an invoice number, dates, both parties' details,
      your VAT registration number, a description of the supply, and the net, VAT and gross amounts per rate.
      This page gives the numbers; your invoicing system makes the document.
    </p>

    <h2>Margin is always on the net figure</h2>
    <p>
      The VAT you charge is collected for the tax authority and passes through your business. It is not revenue
      or profit, and it must never appear in a margin calculation. Set prices and calculate margin on the net
      figure, and add VAT last.
    </p>

    <h2>Registration thresholds and the flat-rate trap</h2>
    <p>
      Most VAT systems have a registration threshold; below it you do not charge or reclaim VAT. Just above it,
      a business that sells mainly to consumers loses the VAT fraction from its prices unless it raises them.
      For this reason many businesses keep their turnover just below the threshold. In business-to-business
      sales the effect mostly disappears, because your customers reclaim what you charge.
    </p>

    <h2>Runs in your browser</h2>
    <p>
      Invoice values, client work and turnover figures stay in this browser tab. Your device makes the CSV,
      Excel and PDF exports, and the page works with the network off.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
